    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"This CD-ROM contains copies of the navigation and field sidescan-sonar data collected aboard the R/V Neecho during a research cruise in Lake Titicaca, Bolivia/Peru. The field program was conducted from 7 January - 2 February, 1999, and was a collaborative field program involving the USGS Woods Hole Field Center, Duke University, and Syracuse University.\r\n\r\nLake Titicaca, located within Bolivia and Peru, South America (69∞30'W, 16∞S) contains what is believed to be one of the most significant climatic archives in all of South America, and has been identified as one of the primary sites for completion of the Pole-Equator-Pole (PEP) paleoclimatic transect of the Western Hemisphere. Preliminary analysis of piston cores and reconnaissance CHIRP subbottom data demonstrated that the sedimentary components necessary for a successful paleoclimatic reconstruction are present and generally well-preserved. However, detailed geophysical data that would provide the critical framework for the interpretation of existing data and planning of future work are lacking.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
